A||N||11||87ECO5||Another Air Filter Question||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||07-12-2000||04:22 PM||||Yes, here we go again.  Another Duke owner trying to squeeze a few more ponies out of their engine!  Actually, I'm going more for looks than power!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Anyway, I'm wondering if these types of filters are available for the Fiero:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/427_filter.jpg"> <P>If they are, would it be a good or bad thing to install one (I wasn't sure how performance would be impacted by the intake pulling in REALLY hot air)?<P>Just curious ...||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||07-12-2000||04:28 PM||||Fiero uses a cold air intake. Eliminating it would hurt performance. Infact on a 4 cyl adding some smalle heat sheilds where the air duct passes over the exhaust would help a bit. <P>Why? At idle the air moves slower and the air duct routing over the exhaust can raise incomming air temp 30-50 degrees. I found this out by watching the MAT sensor on my 87. I was suprised how warm even in the winter that the duct work gets. At higher speed the air isn't warmed as much since it moves faster.<P>This was kind of a suprise when I saw it. I thought one of the problems I was getting was Icing in the TBI, but when the MAT is showing 100+ air temps there's just not much chance the TBI is iced.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||07-12-2000||08:21 PM||||That type of air filter definitely won't fit your Fiero, Scott - it will only fit 1/25 scale models of Corvettes!<P>Seriously, is that one of your models?  That engine compartment looks fantastic.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||07-12-2000||08:29 PM||||RATS!  You can tell it's a model!?  You're not supposed to be able to ...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
